---
import TechniquePost from "../../layouts/TechniquePost.astro"
import ToastBuild from "../../components/UnderTheHood/ToastBuild.astro"
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components"
---

<TechniquePost slug="toast-css-lifecycle">
  <section>
    <h2 id="the-problem">The problem</h2>
    <p>
      A toast enters, waits a few seconds and leaves, and the stack around it
      moves to make room. That usually means a <code>setTimeout</code> per toast,
      measuring heights for the stack, and clearing and restarting timers to pause
      on hover.
    </p>
    <p>
      CSS already has a clock that pauses, and now it can animate a height from
      zero. The whole lifecycle fits in CSS.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="the-trick">The trick</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        A registered <code>--presence</code> number, <code>0</code> for gone and <code
          >1</code
        > for here. Opacity, slide, margins and height all derive from it
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>@starting-style</code> starts a new toast at <code>0</code>, and a
        transition takes it to <code>1</code>
      </li>
      <li>
        The lifetime is the delay of an exit animation that takes <code
          >--presence</code
        > back to <code>0</code>. <code>animation-play-state: paused</code> on hover
        and focus stops the clock
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>calc-size(auto, size * var(--presence))</code> grows and shrinks the
        height, so the stack moves instead of jumping
      </li>
      <li>
        JS only appends toasts and removes them on <code>animationend</code>, no
        timers
      </li>
    </ul>
    <Code
      lang="css"
      code={`@property --presence {
  syntax: "<number>";
  inherits: false;
  initial-value: 1;
}

.toast {
  animation: toast-expire 0.2s var(--duration, 5s) forwards;
  block-size: calc-size(auto, size * var(--presence));
  opacity: var(--presence);
  overflow: clip;
  transition: --presence 0.3s;

  @starting-style {
    --presence: 0;
  }
}

.toaster:is(:hover, :focus-within) .toast {
  animation-play-state: paused;
}

@keyframes toast-expire {
  to {
    --presence: 0;
    visibility: hidden;
  }
}`}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="try-it">Try it</h2>
    <ToastBuild />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="fallback">Fallback</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Browsers without <code>calc-size()</code> drop the <code
          >block-size</code
        >
        {" "}declaration and keep <code>auto</code>
      </li>
      <li>
        Toasts still fade, slide, pause and leave. The stack snaps into place
        instead of sliding
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</TechniquePost>
